ICE / Blacknight
have their services accessible only via VPN.

they gave us cidr blocks of public IPs they use for their services.

our solution (in AWS):
Site to site VPN as following:

Vpc called VpnHub:
Api GW - public - secured with api token.
2x AZ and, 2x Private subnet, 2x private NAT.
VGW attached VPN. 
Lambda forwarder (not true proxy) attached to VPC (two private subnets)

api gw endpoint is consumed by sbaas service(not part of this solution) with POST request passing body of this shape:
(plus some other values)
```
{
	"endpoint": "https://some.domain.1234ice.com",
	"agentOptions": {
		"keepAlive": true,
		"maxSockets": 10,
	}
}

```

In order to test our Site to site VPN setup I have build a simulated vpn device in separate VPC called VpnSim
it contains:
- EC2 instance running libreswan to simulate the VPN on ICEs side.

- EC2 backend instance to simulate the backend behind vpn. (with private address 10.200.1.246)
python server is running on port 8080

for sumulation I don't use actual cidr block but testing cidr 203.0.113.0/24 mimick the ICEs cidr block they gave us.

all routing at the moment does work if we use IP address to reach BE service:

{
	"endpoint": "https://203.0.113.246:8080",
	"agentOptions": {
		"keepAlive": true,
		"maxSockets": 10,
	}
}


